<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Describe a shared study population with one column per site and a total,
using baseline values, common categories and explicit missing counts.
</div>

**You need:** the Opal login from [Getting started](book-00-start.html) and
[the lab lesson](book-14-labs.html). This chapter uses the two Synthea sites.

## Research question

What are the age, sex and baseline haemoglobin characteristics of people entering
at their first haemoglobin measurement? We build the descriptive table that opens
most study reports, often called “Table 1”. The two Synthea sites contain disjoint
halves of the same people, so their people can be added without duplication.

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
two_conns <- ds.omop.login(rep("https://localhost:8443", 2), user = "administrator",
  password = "password",
  server = c("north", "south"),
  resource = c(north = "omop.synthea_north", south = "omop.synthea_south"))$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
two_conns <- book_connect("numeric", two_sites = TRUE)$conns
```

## Confirm the measurement and units at both sites

```{r table-discovery, table.row.names = FALSE}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Hemoglobin", domain = "Measurement", datasources = two_conns)$pooled
selected <- hits[trimws(hits$concept_name) == "Hemoglobin [Mass/volume] in Blood", ]
selected[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept", "vocabulary_id")]
hb_id <- selected$concept_id
unit_counts <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id", concept_id = hb_id,
  datasources = two_conns)$per_site
do.call(rbind, lapply(names(unit_counts), function(site)
  data.frame(Site = site, unit_counts[[site]][c("value", "concept_name", "n")])))
hb_set <- omop_concept_set(hb_id)
saveRDS(hb_set, "baseline-haemoglobin.rds")
```

The shared concept is **3000963**, and both sites record **8713**, gram per
deciliter (g/dL). Each site reports the band 50–54 rows. Adding the band floors
gives 100; it does not mean there are exactly 100 measurements. Meaning and units
must agree before pooling, not merely the displayed name.

## Use only information available at entry

Completed age is measured at the first haemoglobin date. Choose haemoglobin on
that date, with no positive tolerance; an all-history mean would include future
values and would not describe baseline. Sex is the recorded female/male category;
`omop_sex()` represents it as F/M, labelled Female/Male in the report below.

```{r baseline-recipe}
hb <- omop_event("measurement", hb_set, unit = omop_unit(8713L))
characterise <- omop_study(hb, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  age = omop_age_at("index"), sex = omop_sex(),
  age_band = omop_number_bins(omop_age_at("index"), breaks = c(0, 18, 121),
    labels = c("under 18", "adult")),
  haemoglobin = omop_value_at_index("measurement", hb_set,
    tolerance = c(-90, 0), unit = omop_unit(8713L))))
result <- recipe_execute(characterise, out = c(baseline = "D_baseline"), datasources = two_conns)
columns <- recipe_codebook(result)[[1]]$columns
age_column <- match("age_band", columns$name)
data.frame(Age_group = columns$levels[[age_column]]$labels)
```

Both sites use the declared age groups under 18 and adult (published as `under_18` and
`adult`, labels that every dsBase function can carry), including any unused
level. The same definition and index are required for comparable columns.
The 90-day lookback cannot add earlier haemoglobin here because entry is the
first haemoglobin; its selected value is the baseline reading itself.

## Assemble the descriptive table from released aggregates

For each numeric variable, `ds.mean()` supplies its mean and nonmissing count;
`ds.var()` supplies variance, whose square root is the standard deviation (SD).
For categories, percentages use all people in that site, with missingness shown
as its own row. No patient rows are combined locally.

```{r descriptive-table, table.row.names = FALSE}
describe <- function(connections) {
  aggregate_mean <- function(column) dsBaseClient::ds.mean(paste0("D_baseline$", column),
    type = "combine", datasources = connections)$Global.Mean
  variance <- function(column) dsBaseClient::ds.var(paste0("D_baseline$", column),
    type = "combine", datasources = connections)$Global.Variance
  age <- aggregate_mean("age"); lab <- aggregate_mean("haemoglobin")
  n <- age[1, "Ntotal"]
  numeric_text <- function(x, v) sprintf("%.2f (%.2f)", x[1, "EstimatedMean"], sqrt(v[1, "EstimatedVar"]))
  counts <- function(column) dsBaseClient::ds.table(paste0("D_baseline$", column),
    useNA = "always", datasources = connections)$output.list$TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts
  sex <- counts("sex"); bands <- counts("age_band")
  cell <- function(numerator) sprintf("%d (%.1f%%)", numerator, 100 * numerator / n)
  c(People = as.character(n),
    `Age, years: mean (SD)` = numeric_text(age, variance("age")),
    `Age missing, n` = as.character(age[1, "Nmissing"]),
    `Female, n (%)` = cell(sex[["F"]]), `Male, n (%)` = cell(sex[["M"]]),
    `Sex missing, n (%)` = cell(sex[["NA"]]),
    `Under 18, n (%)` = cell(bands[["under_18"]]), `Adult, n (%)` = cell(bands[["adult"]]),
    `Age group missing, n (%)` = cell(bands[["NA"]]),
    `Baseline haemoglobin, g/dL: mean (SD)` = numeric_text(lab, variance("haemoglobin")),
    `Haemoglobin missing, n` = as.character(lab[1, "Nmissing"]))
}
site_a <- describe(two_conns[1])
site_b <- describe(two_conns[2])
total <- describe(two_conns)
data.frame(Characteristic = names(total), north = unname(site_a),
  south = unname(site_b), Total = unname(total))
```

Each site contributes 14 people, for 28 total. The sites differ: female counts are
4 (28.6%) and 9 (64.3%). Mean completed age is 18.64 and 25.36 years, with pooled
mean 22.00. Pooled baseline haemoglobin is **13.99 g/dL**, not the all-history
mean 14.47. Missing rows state the denominator for each variable; a withheld
cell must never be silently filled with zero.

The total SD includes both within-site and between-site variation. It is not
obtained by averaging site SDs. Site columns make population differences visible
that a pooled column alone would hide.

## Exercises

**1. Detect future information.** Replace baseline haemoglobin with the mean
over all history, keeping the same first-haemoglobin population. Quantify the difference.

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r future-information, table.digits = 3}
all_history <- omop_study(hb, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  baseline = omop_value_at_index("measurement", hb_set, tolerance = c(0, 0), unit = omop_unit(8713L)),
  history = omop_variable(event = hb, reducer = omop_reduce("mean"))))
recipe_execute(all_history, out = c(baseline = "D_history"), datasources = two_conns)
ds.omop.mutate("D_history", difference = history - baseline,
  newobj = "D_history_difference", datasources = two_conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.mean("D_history_difference$difference", type = "combine", datasources = two_conns)$Global.Mean
```

The mean difference is about 0.481 g/dL. The all-history mean includes later
readings for 25 people and therefore cannot be used as a baseline characteristic.

</details>

**2. Add a common haemoglobin category.** Use a public teaching cutoff of 14 g/dL
and show the counts separately at each site. Do equal definitions imply equal counts?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r baseline-bands}
bands <- omop_study(hb, outputs = "baseline", baseline = list(
  band = omop_number_bins(omop_value_at_index("measurement", hb_set,
    tolerance = c(0, 0), unit = omop_unit(8713L)),
    breaks = c(-Inf, 14, Inf), labels = c("below 14", "14 or above"))))
recipe_execute(bands, out = c(baseline = "D_baseline_bands"), datasources = two_conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_baseline_bands$band",
  datasources = two_conns)$output.list$TABLE_rvar.by.study_counts
```

Both sites use the same boundary and labels. Counts depend on their people;
sharing a definition makes comparison meaningful without forcing distributions
to match. The cutoff is a teaching rule, not a clinical reference interval.

</details>

## Recap

Describe the population at a shared entry point, keep future information out of
baseline variables, and show each site beside the total. Report missingness and
use pooled variance for the total SD.

**Next:** revisit the [capability tour](book-showcase.html) to choose the next analysis for your study.
